1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,839
A fast fan looks like a blurred disc.

2
00:00:02,839 --> 00:00:05,219
A bee keeps buzzing around one flower.

3
00:00:05,219 --> 00:00:08,229
A swing in a night photo
becomes a smooth smear.

4
00:00:08,629 --> 00:00:11,549
In each one, we cannot
follow the thing itself.

5
00:00:11,549 --> 00:00:13,929
We only see where it is likely to be.

6
00:00:14,379 --> 00:00:16,298
Diya sees it in her kitchen.

7
00:00:16,298 --> 00:00:18,160
Steam rises from the cooker.

8
00:00:18,160 --> 00:00:21,941
She cannot follow one droplet,
only the shape of the cloud.

9
00:00:22,291 --> 00:00:23,793
Electrons are like that.

10
00:00:23,793 --> 00:00:27,927
We cannot track one, so chemists draw
the shape of the space where it is

11
00:00:27,927 --> 00:00:28,887
likely to be.

12
00:00:29,387 --> 00:00:33,735
By the end of this video, you will know
why a p orbital looks like a dumbbell,

13
00:00:33,735 --> 00:00:35,334
with nothing at its centre.

14
00:00:35,984 --> 00:00:38,139
So what does an orbital look like?

15
00:00:38,489 --> 00:00:42,164
Imagine marking a dot at every
place the electron might be.

16
00:00:42,164 --> 00:00:45,154
Where the dots are thick,
the chance is high.

17
00:00:45,154 --> 00:00:48,858
This chance is the probability
density, written psi squared.

18
00:00:49,258 --> 00:00:53,282
Now draw a boundary that holds
about ninety percent of the dots.

19
00:00:53,282 --> 00:00:55,559
This is a boundary surface diagram.

20
00:00:55,559 --> 00:00:57,846
It gives the shape of the orbital.

21
00:00:58,446 --> 00:01:01,134
Let's see the shapes
on our molecule bench.

22
00:01:01,134 --> 00:01:02,645
Start with s orbitals.

23
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:05,529
One s is a sphere around the nucleus.

24
00:01:05,529 --> 00:01:08,004
Drawn flat, it looks like a circle.

25
00:01:08,004 --> 00:01:09,919
Every direction is the same.

26
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:12,100
Two s is a bigger sphere.

27
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:16,793
But inside it, there is a thin shell
where the chance of finding the electron

28
00:01:16,793 --> 00:01:17,693
is zero.

29
00:01:17,531 --> 00:01:18,718
That is a node.

30
00:01:19,118 --> 00:01:22,391
A node shaped like a
sphere is a radial node.

31
00:01:22,391 --> 00:01:24,408
Two s has one radial node.

32
00:01:24,408 --> 00:01:25,770
Three s has two.

33
00:01:26,170 --> 00:01:27,323
Now p orbitals.

34
00:01:27,323 --> 00:01:32,210
Each one has two lobes, one on each
side of the nucleus, like a dumbbell.

35
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:36,884
Between the lobes is a flat plane
through the nucleus, where the chance

36
00:01:36,884 --> 00:01:37,784
is zero.

37
00:01:37,618 --> 00:01:40,956
This is an angular node,
also called a nodal plane.

38
00:01:41,356 --> 00:01:44,715
There are three p
orbitals, along x, y and z.

39
00:01:44,715 --> 00:01:47,432
Same size, same shape and same energy.

40
00:01:47,432 --> 00:01:49,519
They differ only in direction.

41
00:01:49,919 --> 00:01:51,218
Next, d orbitals.

42
00:01:51,218 --> 00:01:53,986
For l equals two, there are five of them.

43
00:01:53,986 --> 00:01:56,697
The first four look
like four leaf clovers.

44
00:01:57,097 --> 00:02:02,137
In d x y, d y z and d z x, the
lobes lie between the axes.

45
00:02:02,137 --> 00:02:06,786
In d x squared minus y squared,
the lobes lie on the axes.

46
00:02:07,186 --> 00:02:09,909
The fifth, d z squared, looks different.

47
00:02:09,909 --> 00:02:13,135
Two lobes along z, with
a ring around the middle.

48
00:02:13,135 --> 00:02:16,599
Still, all five three d
orbitals have the same energy.

49
00:02:17,249 --> 00:02:19,428
Now look at two s and two p.

50
00:02:19,428 --> 00:02:21,588
Each one has exactly one node.

51
00:02:21,988 --> 00:02:23,071
Pause and think.

52
00:02:23,071 --> 00:02:26,109
Which one has a radial
node, shaped like a sphere?

53
00:02:29,309 --> 00:02:30,209
Two s.

54
00:02:30,002 --> 00:02:31,655
Its node is a sphere.

55
00:02:31,655 --> 00:02:35,168
Two p has a flat plane
through the nucleus instead.

56
00:02:35,168 --> 00:02:36,976
That is an angular node.

57
00:02:37,626 --> 00:02:40,223
So how many nodes does an orbital have?

58
00:02:40,223 --> 00:02:42,470
Let's count, one step at a time.

59
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:43,720
Step one.

60
00:02:43,638 --> 00:02:46,401
Angular nodes, the flat ones, equal l.

61
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:49,502
So s has zero, p has one, and d has two.

62
00:02:50,252 --> 00:02:51,158
Step two.

63
00:02:51,158 --> 00:02:55,474
Radial nodes, the round ones,
equal n minus l minus one.

64
00:02:56,224 --> 00:02:57,191
Step three.

65
00:02:57,191 --> 00:02:58,091
Add them.

66
00:02:58,053 --> 00:03:01,374
l plus n minus l minus
one gives n minus one.

67
00:03:01,374 --> 00:03:03,866
That is the total number of nodes.

68
00:03:04,616 --> 00:03:05,561
Step four.

69
00:03:05,561 --> 00:03:06,680
Check two p.

70
00:03:06,680 --> 00:03:09,919
Radial, two minus one minus one, is zero.

71
00:03:09,919 --> 00:03:11,201
Angular is one.

72
00:03:11,201 --> 00:03:13,885
Total one, which is two minus one.

73
00:03:14,635 --> 00:03:15,923
Remember this.

74
00:03:15,923 --> 00:03:17,835
Angular nodes equal l.

75
00:03:17,835 --> 00:03:21,059
Radial nodes equal n minus l minus one.

76
00:03:21,759 --> 00:03:23,495
Now, one worked example.

77
00:03:23,795 --> 00:03:27,550
Count the nodes of a three d
orbital, and a four p orbital.

78
00:03:28,050 --> 00:03:28,950
Three d.

79
00:03:28,823 --> 00:03:30,699
n is three and l is two.

80
00:03:30,699 --> 00:03:32,184
Angular nodes, two.

81
00:03:32,184 --> 00:03:35,425
Radial nodes, three minus
two minus one, zero.

82
00:03:36,175 --> 00:03:39,195
So all the nodes of
three d are flat planes.

83
00:03:39,195 --> 00:03:41,740
Total two, which is three minus one.

84
00:03:42,490 --> 00:03:43,390
Four p.

85
00:03:43,234 --> 00:03:45,114
n is four and l is one.

86
00:03:45,114 --> 00:03:46,643
Angular nodes, one.

87
00:03:46,643 --> 00:03:49,877
Radial nodes, four minus
one minus one, two.

88
00:03:50,627 --> 00:03:53,319
Total three, which is four minus one.

89
00:03:53,319 --> 00:03:54,737
Both answers check.

90
00:03:55,387 --> 00:03:56,853
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:03:57,203 --> 00:04:00,731
Many students say radial
nodes equal n minus one.

92
00:04:00,731 --> 00:04:03,431
So they give two p one radial node.

93
00:04:03,831 --> 00:04:06,187
But n minus one is the total.

94
00:04:06,187 --> 00:04:09,054
Radial nodes are n minus l minus one.

95
00:04:09,054 --> 00:04:11,121
Two p has no radial node.

96
00:04:11,121 --> 00:04:13,643
Its only node is the flat plane.

97
00:04:14,293 --> 00:04:15,795
Where is this asked?

98
00:04:15,795 --> 00:04:20,462
In JEE Main, as counting radial and
angular nodes, or naming the orbital

99
00:04:20,462 --> 00:04:21,651
from its nodes.

100
00:04:22,251 --> 00:04:23,533
Exam level now.

101
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:27,336
An orbital has two radial
nodes and one angular node.

102
00:04:27,336 --> 00:04:28,955
Which orbital is it?

103
00:04:28,955 --> 00:04:30,291
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:35,691 --> 00:04:37,266
The answer is B.

105
00:04:37,266 --> 00:04:38,166
Four p.

106
00:04:38,645 --> 00:04:41,323
One angular node means l equals one.

107
00:04:41,323 --> 00:04:43,123
So it is a p orbital.

108
00:04:43,623 --> 00:04:47,311
Radial nodes are n minus
l minus one, which is two.

109
00:04:47,311 --> 00:04:50,419
So n minus two equals two, and n is four.

110
00:04:50,919 --> 00:04:51,819
Four p.

111
00:04:51,724 --> 00:04:55,289
Option A, three p, has
only one radial node.

112
00:04:55,889 --> 00:04:57,351
Here is your recap.

113
00:04:57,351 --> 00:04:58,854
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:02,054 --> 00:05:05,053
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:05,453 --> 00:05:06,353
One.

116
00:05:05,956 --> 00:05:10,813
What shape is an s orbital, and how
does it change from one s to two s?

117
00:05:11,613 --> 00:05:12,513
Two.

118
00:05:12,182 --> 00:05:15,568
How many angular nodes
does a d orbital have?

119
00:05:16,368 --> 00:05:17,268
Three.

120
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,472
How many radial nodes does
a three s orbital have?

121
00:05:21,372 --> 00:05:26,615
Next time: three d and four s are close
in energy, so which one fills first?

122
00:05:26,615 --> 00:05:31,416
We find out in Aufbau principle, Pauli
exclusion principle and Hund's rule.
